# Logical negation of a dynamic element

Verifies `!` binds its operand tighter than the element access, so the logical
negation applies to the extracted element rather than to the array. The first
stored element is `true`, so the negation is `false`, and the conjunction of the
element with itself stays `true`.

>>> source
let flags: bool[] = [true, false]
let index = 0
print(!flags[index])
print(flags[index] && flags[index])

<<< stdout
false
true

<<< stderr
